# Port patch, a new cheat "Bloody mode" (1.1.3, the project owner: blood pools on the floor stay instead of vanishing
# after a few seconds). The game's floor blood is the ground decal sprite (effect id 0x10, cEsp10): it grows, holds and
# fades (cEsp::ColorUpdate), or ends with its life or its texture animation. Which decals are blood: the same textures and
# sequences make dust, dirt and footprints, so the blood helpers' control block of patch 0256 marks them. With the switch
# on, the blood helpers of a hit (patch 0256's Re4BloodScope) and the Ganados' death pool (em10.cpp EFF_EM10 0x18, here
# in a scope without an enemy, so Berserker does not make it bigger) hand their effects a neutral block (no change) with
# the blood mark; EspSeqSet (esp_sub.cpp) asks runtime/bloody.cpp re4_bloody_rec, which marks a floor decal made under it,
# and then re4_berserker_blood_rec as before. cEsp10::move asks re4_bloody_move first: in a marked decal's first frame it
# decides, and it moves a kept pool itself (the game's CommonMove without fade or end of life, the animation holding its
# last picture). The room's next load clears the sprite pool and with it the pools. At most 160 pools are kept (64 and
# 32 with Potato mode's fewer effects; each pool in sight is one more draw), the oldest released first, fewer while the pool of 1024 sprites has less than 256 free, and kept pools carry the game's own
# low-priority mark (Tool_flg 0x40000), which the sprite pool takes first when it is full: muzzle flashes, sparks and the
# rest never run short. Off: as the game.
